## FINDING 0 (T2, ops) — the miner is not auth-blocked, it is being OOM-killed, by banned ground

The 08-26 log's #1 owed item was "RESTORE THE REGIONAL SEAT AUTH — seven straight days of
131-byte stubs is the largest single suppressor of this desk's arrival rate". **That is no longer
the failure.** Today's two unified runs (0620, 0800) both passed `brain_auth_check` — their logs
contain the `start` line, which is written *after* the auth gate. What they do NOT contain is the
`exit` line, and that line is unconditional. A missing unconditional line means the process group
was SIGKILLed.

Cause, measured: the box is 3814MB with **no swap** and had **298MB free**. Resident at the time:
`memecoin-shadow --dry-run` (367MB, systemd user unit) and
`scripts/run_cashcarry_executor.py --live --top 10 --capital 4500` (30h uptime, 144MB). **~510MB
held by two processes on the crypto-exchange ground the 2026-08-18 mandate bans**, on a box where
that is the difference between a miner running and a miner dying. `memecoin-shadow` was itself
OOM-killed at 00:09:36 today, which is the corroborating record.

**NOT ACTED ON.** The cashcarry process is LIVE CAPITAL and LAWS §4 reserves arming and disarming
to the principal; a research organ that stops a live executor to free RAM has done the exact thing
the firewall exists to prevent. Ledgered **R0676** with the two-step principal act (confirm the
book is flat then stop the unit; `systemctl --user disable --now memecoin-shadow`).

**The honest limit on this finding:** kernel-log channels are UNREADABLE from this box (R0350), so
I cannot produce a kill record for the *miner* processes specifically. The OOM is inferred from the
missing unconditional exit line plus the memory arithmetic plus a confirmed OOM of a neighbour
