as one.

## FINDING 1 (T1, the cycle's find) — OP-098: Wayback CDX is a POPULATION ENUMERATOR, and it
## reopens two grounds the desk had recorded as dead

A track-record ground has two independent routes: **per-entity** (one trader's page) and
**population** (who exists). Sites routinely leave the first open and hide the second. The standing
failure is to confirm per-entity, fail to find a listing, and stop — which is verbatim where the
08-25 EN dig stopped on FX Blue: *"sitemap.xml enumerates exactly ONE user (`/users/example`) —
there is no population route, and a track-record ground without a population cannot be mined."*

That verdict was wrong and the correction is one keyless command. The archive's URL index is a
population enumerator; it returns identifiers only, so every subsequent fetch goes to the live
origin and archive staleness cannot contaminate the data.

**Measured, both this session:** `fxblue.com/users/*` → **5,077 handles**;
`darwinex.com/invest/*` → **1,479 DARWIN tickers**. Both grounds were on the books as
population-blocked. Written up as **OP-098** with the recipe, the two real caveats (path-prefix CDX
is subdomain-blind; robots is not a reuse grant), the standing target list (Myfxbook, Collective2,
ZuluTrade, eToro) and MQL5 named as the CONTROL — it has a natively-enumerated population, so
running CDX there validates the operator against a known answer. **Corollary now standing under
L1.51: "no population route" is a claim that requires the CDX probe, and it costs one request.**

## FINDING 2 (T1) — FX Blue is fully open, and it is a black-box reverse-engineering corpus

Walked the whole data layer keyless. The 08-25 dig had stopped at "the per-trade XHR endpoint is
not in scripts.aspx nor the preloaded chunks"; the route was in `wl/strive/strive_account.js`,
a bundle that read had not opened.

- population — CDX, 5,077 handles (above)
- liveness   — `api.fxblue.com/strivewidget.aspx?displayUserId=<u>`
- headline   — `api.fxblue.com/wl/view.aspx?id=<uid>&mode=overview&isinline=1` → balance, equity,
               closed profit, currency, real-vs-demo, last update
- mechanism  — `api.fxblue.com/wl/charts/<chart>.aspx?id=<uid>`, **51 charts**, numbers literal in
               a Google-Charts `addRows([...])` block

One false lead recorded so nobody re-walks it: `_StatsData.aspx` looks like the data endpoint and
is a CSV **export sink** (client→server POST). It is not a source.

The 51 charts are the point, because they are precisely the §4 black-box axes and they are
published: **hour-of-day** (session bias), **symbol** (instrument set), **duration/profitability**
(holding-time structure), **direction** (long/short asymmetry), **day-of-week**, **lots by symbol**
(sizing habits), **balance drawdown %** (drawdown geometry). This reconstructs a published trader's
MECHANISM without ever touching a trade — which is what §4 asks for and what copying is not.

§13: `www.fxblue.com/robots.txt` is `Allow: /`; `api.fxblue.com` serves no robots. Recorded with
the OP-096 caveat that robots is not a reuse grant — this is internal research, never redistribution.

**Wired, not just built:** `desks/mt5/scripts/fxblue_track_record_miner.py` (rotation cursor +
stride, resumable, polite) and `desks/mt5/scripts/fxblue_mechanism_summary.py`.

## FINDING 3 (T2) — my own first harvest was a false positive, and the population is block-structured

First run graded **95/120 "live"** at offset 400. Every one of them was **zero-filled**: a
contiguous run of bulk-registered `22-*` handles, balance 0.0, last update 2022. I had defined
liveness as *the widget returned >2000 bytes* — the desk's own lesson that **a 200 and a byte count
are not content**, reproduced by me in the same session I quoted it.

Two repairs, both in the code rather than in a note:
1. **Liveness is `has_data`** — a non-zero number in a mechanism chart — and `shell` is kept as its
   own status. A shell is a real account with nothing to mine; that is neither a fetch failure nor
   a mineable record and the consumer must not collapse the three.
2. **Sampling is STRIDED.** A contiguous slice of a block-structured population measures one block,
   not the population.

Re-measured on a 117-handle stride sample: **83 data-bearing / 27 shell / 10 dead ≈ 71%** ⇒
**~3,600 mineable track records**, against the ~100% my broken test had claimed.

## FINDING 4 (T2) — first read of the corpus: MT5-native, and one number that is NOT a finding

Instrument mix (accounts trading each symbol, n=83): EURUSD 50.6%, GBPUSD 41.0%, USDJPY 34.9%,
AUDUSD 31.3%, USDCHF 26.5%, USDCAD 25.3%, EURJPY 22.9%, **XAUUSD 21.7%**. Entirely inside the
MT5/Fusion mandate — this ground needs no translation layer, which is exactly what the crypto-era
corpora did need.

Activity clock (per-account normalised, so one whale cannot set it): peaks **15:00–17:00**
(London/NY overlap, 7.45%/6.79%/7.36%) and **09:00–11:00** (London open). **A caveat I am recording
rather than reading as a result:** 00:00 shows 6.66%, higher than any hour around it, which has the
shape of a null/rollover bucket rather than Sydney-open activity. Untested; do not build on it.

Survivorship-conditioned profitable-share by hour peaks at 15:00 (63.9%) and 16:00 (60.2%) against
a ~50% base. **This is not a finding and I am not carding it as one.** At n=83 the binomial SE is
~5.5pp, so 63.9% is ~2.5 SE from the null on a **24-hour scan** — multiplicity alone accounts for
it. It is also not independent of the activity clock: the same hours carry the most trades. The
population is maximally survivorship-biased (§4, master 23). The summariser prints ACTIVITY and
PERFORMANCE under separate headings with that warning inline, because the tempting mistake here is
to read a survivor-conditioned hour bucket as a session edge.

## FINDING 5 (T2) — TradingView is KILLED on §13, and a one-group robots read inverts the verdict

`tradingview.com/robots.txt` carries two user-agent groups. The `*` group bars almost nothing
relevant. A **second group enumerates 16 AI crawlers — including `ClaudeBot`** — and gives that
group `Disallow: /ideas/*, /scripts/*, /script/*, /v/*, /u/*, /chat/*, /chart/*, /watchlists/*`.
The publisher has specifically withheld this exact ground from this exact class of agent. Seeds S12
(card 52) and S3 (card 43) are the same ground described twice and are **recorded as ONE kill**,
with a graveyard entry and named re-open triggers (robots change / published research API /
explicit written permission — a human in a browser is not a re-open trigger and does not become one
at scale). No UA-substitution route will be built.

This is the false-POSITIVE twin of the KR-s5 lesson: read only the `*` group and TradingView looks
wide open. **Group-scope every robots read against the desk's own agent identity, never `*`.**

